WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.560
Here, below London, 32km of new tunnels are
nearly finished.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.660
But these aren't for people: they're for power.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.049
Cables carrying 400,000 volts will run through
here,

00:00:12.049 --> 00:00:13.549
supporting the city as it keeps growing

00:00:13.549 --> 00:00:16.550
and keeps demanding more and more electricity.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.670
This project cost a billion pounds,

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:20.810
all to keep the lights on.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.000
So how did we get to this?

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.720
Back in the 1920s, electricity was generated
by hundreds of small companies

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.769
in towns and cities across the country.

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:32.630
They were all different and mostly incompatible:

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:36.470
London alone had 24 voltages and 10 frequencies.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.760
So if you moved to the next street over,

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.010
you might have to replace all your light bulbs.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.550
If you had any light bulbs, that is:

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.240
the cost of electricity was out of reach of
nearly everyone.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.710
Now the answer might have been to do what
other countries in Europe were doing:

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.900
set up and enforce standards, regulate prices,

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.030
maybe even nationalise the power companies.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:56.770
And bring the price down for everyone --

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.620
people and business. Good plan in theory,

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.750
but the trouble was that the Prime Minister
at the time was Stanley Baldwin,

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.110
and he led the Conservative party. Politically,

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.740
he couldn't just assume control of private
corporations!

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:11.870
That was socialist.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.470
That was something that the Other Side did.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:19.750
So the solution was a very clever and very
British compromise:

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.430
the National Gridiron, later just National
Grid.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.340
The Central Electricity Board,

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.000
backed up by the government and run privately,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.270
would construct and own the central infrastructure.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:35.130
It would build a network of transmission lines
sweeping across the country.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:38.590
The Grid would reduce the risk for the private
generating companies

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.890
and let them reach a much, much, larger market.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:42.890
If they wanted to connect and reach everyone,

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.840
they had to meet the Grid's standards for
frequency,

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.900
for voltage, and for reliability.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.180
The greatest story I found while I was researching
this is that

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.150
originally, there were six separate grid areas

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.630
spread all around the country, all run individually.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.260
It was judged too risky by their managers

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.210
to actually connect them all into one National
Grid.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.430
But it was theoretically possible.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:10.749
Sometimes two or maybe even three of those
grid areas were connected

00:02:10.749 --> 00:02:13.499
to help out if one power station somewhere
went down.

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:19.040
So on the night of 29th October, 1937, without
any permission,

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.920
the engineers in the control rooms quietly
decided

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.969
to connect the whole country together for
an hour or two

00:02:24.969 --> 00:02:26.150
and see what would happen.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.139
And it worked. And a year later,

00:02:28.139 --> 00:02:30.230
even their managers agreed: it was the only way

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:33.499
to make sure the lights stayed on everywhere.

00:02:33.499 --> 00:02:36.129
And so while the level of regulation,

00:02:36.129 --> 00:02:37.389
and the level of privatisation,

00:02:37.389 --> 00:02:38.870
has changed over the years --

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:42.180
as it has in pretty much every country in
the world --

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.810
and while the technology has developed to
the point

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:47.019
that instead of laying transmission lines,

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:51.499
we are now in billion-pound tunnelling projects
60m under London,

00:02:51.499 --> 00:02:54.109
that mostly-private grid is what we still
have today.

00:02:54.109 --> 00:02:56.359
It's listed on the London Stock Exchange.

00:02:56.359 --> 00:02:59.379
If history had turned out differently,

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:01.809
perhaps this tunnel would have been a government
operation:

00:03:01.809 --> 00:03:03.579
but even if it was,

00:03:03.579 --> 00:03:06.730
we'd have still needed this to keep the lights
on.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.579
Thank you very much to the National Grid

00:03:08.579 --> 00:03:11.680
and all the folks who've helped bring me down
to the Power Tunnels,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.409
along with Geoff from Londonist,

00:03:13.409 --> 00:03:15.969
who's done a video about these tunnels themselves

00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:17.599
and all the infrastructure around me.

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:18.790
Go check that out on his channel!

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.520
